How to reproduce:; ; 1) Start eclipse; 2) File>>New>>Project>>Java Project>>Create project from existing source; ; Symptoms:; ; Most of the packages will be imported properly.  They will show up in the; package explorer with a package icon and their directory levels will be; separated by periods AKA dots.  These packages work fine.; ; A few of the packages will instead have a package-in-a-folder icon and their; directory levels will be separated by slashes.  Expanding the tree for these; packages shows an entry labeled (default package) which contains the sources; for the package.  The sources are marked with all sorts of false errors; because; Eclipse thinks they have the wrong package declaration.  More concretely if this; happened to the com.foo.bar package; at the top of every source file package; com.foo.bar; would have com.foo.bar underlined saying Declared package does; not match expected package.; ; Sub packages may show up as peers of this (default package) in the tree.  If; so; they will be marked with a package icon; but fail to compile; because; Eclipse wants the same sort of package declaration it thinks the parent package; should have.  In other words; since Eclipse thinks com.foo.bar is the default; package; it will want com.foo.bar.baz to just be baz.; ; Sub packages may also exhibit the exact same problem as their parent packages. ; If so; they will show up as peers to both the correctly imported packages and; their parent package.  They will be wrongly marked with a package-in-a-folder; icon and fail to compile due to package problems etc... ; ; A single misinterpreted package may have sub packages that exhibit both of these; behaviors.; ; Finally; Eclipse will put the classes generated from these problem packages in; the default package rather than in their proper places in the hierarchy.  Jad; verifies what Eclipse thinks its doing.  Note the missing package declaration.; ; // Decompiled by Jad v1.5.8f. Copyright 2001 Pavel Kouznetsov.; // Jad home page: http://www.kpdus.com/jad.html; // Decompiler options: packimports(3) ; // Source File Name:   Foo.java; ; ; public class Foo; {; ;     public Foo();     {;         throw new Error(Unresolved compilation problems: 
	The declared; package does not match the expected package 
	The hierarchy of the type; AtomicTest is inconsistent
	SpreadsheetTools cannot be resolved
);;     }; ;     private int has_20_inconsistent_20_hierarchy;; }

