Using HEAD from master and the following test case; you can see that the foo method is compiled as a problem method when no error is reported to the user. So this makes it impossible for the user to find out when problem methods are generated and this can cause issues at runtime.; ; Add this test inside org.eclipse.jdt.core.tests.compiler.regression.NonFatalErrorTest:; ; public void test007() {; if (this.complianceLevel < ClassFileConstants.JDK1_5) {; 	return;; }; Map customOptions = getCompilerOptions();; customOptions.put(CompilerOptions.OPTION_FatalOptionalError; CompilerOptions.ENABLED);; customOptions.put(CompilerOptions.OPTION_ReportUnusedLocal; CompilerOptions.ERROR);; customOptions.put(CompilerOptions.OPTION_SuppressWarnings; CompilerOptions.ENABLED);; customOptions.put(CompilerOptions.OPTION_SuppressOptionalErrors; CompilerOptions.ENABLED);; customOptions.put(CompilerOptions.OPTION_ReportUnusedWarningToken; CompilerOptions.ERROR);; runConformTest(; 	new String[] { /* test files */; 		X.java;; 		public class X {
 +; 				@SuppressWarnings(\unused\)
 +; 				static void foo() {
 +; 					String s = null;
 +; 					System.out.println(\SUCCESS\);
 +; 				}
 +; 				public static void main(String argv[]) {
 +; 					foo();
 +; 				}
 +; 		}; 	};; 	SUCCESS /* expected output string */;; 	null /* no class libraries */;; 	true;; 	null;; 	customOptions /* custom options */;; 	// compiler results; 	null /* do not check error string */);; }; ; ; and run it. I think this should pass. Right now the filtering of problem for compilation unit regarding @SuppressWarnings is done after the code generation is complete.; I think we should do it for each problem when the problem is being reported in a situation where optional error is treated as fatal; because the fatal nature aborts the current code generation.; ; Right now the only workaround is to disable the option to treat optional errors as fatal.; ; We should never generate a problem method when the corresponding error is not reported to the user.

Build Identifier: Version: Indigo Service Release 1 Build id: 20110916-0149; ; 1) Similarly/oppositely/additionally to what was requested in Bug #197325 I would like to request a special blank line formatting rule for blocks of code with braces to be wrapped with blank lines if they are not immediately wrapped with outer blocks/braces.; ; Eclipse already does it for method declarations blocks I believe; separating/wrapping them with blank lines; but not for inner method blocks of code.; ; For example I often see the code that is not readable due to poor code grouping like this (simplified example):; ; public class C {;   public method m() {;     Integer i = null;;     if (expr1) {;       i = 0;     } else {;       while (expr2) {;         statement;;         Integer j = 0;;         statement;;         return j;;       };       i--;;     };     i++;;   }; }; ; but I would like it to look like (added explanation comments):; ; public class C { // Correct - no blank line below needed as we have indentation (wrapping method).;   public Integer method m() {;     Integer i = null;; ;     if (expr1) { // Blank line above as this block is on the same level with above declaration (no indentation) and thus need to separate them visually.;       return null; // No blank line before return because it is immediately wrapped within braces (there is indentation).;     } else {;       while (expr2) { // Correct - no blank line above as we have indentation (immediate wrapping).;         statement;; ;         Integer j = 0; // 2 request: add blank line before and after variable declaration if it is not in the beginning of block (variable declaration adds importance).;         Integer k = 1; // 2 request: add blank line after variable declaration if it doesnt follow other variable declaration as above declaration.; ;         statement;; ;         return j; // 3 request: Add new line before return (method finalization adds importance).;       } // Blank line below as this block is on the same level with below statement (no indentation) and thus need to separate them visually.; ;       i--;;     } // Blank line below same as above.; ;     i++;;   }; }; ; I believe 1) could be simple to implement with the is there immediate wrapping opening/closing brace rule.; ; Note other two common blank line adding practices which I described in comments above:; 2) Add blank line before return if it doesnt go immediately after opening brace.; 3) Add blank line before and after variable declaration if it doesnt immediately goes after opening brace or is not followed by other declaration (declaration could be grouped; but respect blank lines if they are not).; ; The following simple checkstyle rules (regexps) worked for me to check for such 1) formatting; so maybe those will be helpful in implementation:; ; 	<module name=RegexpMultiline>; 		<property name=format value=\{[
]{2;} />; 		<property name=message value=Following block is indented and begins - no need for blank line. />; 	</module>; ; 	<module name=RegexpMultiline>; 		<property name=format value=[
]{2;}[	]*\} />; 		<property name=message value=This block is indented and ends - no need for blank line. />; 	</module>; ; 	<!-- TODO: Should not require blank line between comment and opening block {; e.g. block with comment above. -->; 	<module name=RegexpMultiline>; 		<property name=format value=[;\}][
][^\}
]+?\{[
] />; 		<property name=message value=Following block should be separated with blank line. />; 	</module>; ; 	<module name=RegexpMultiline>; 		<property name=format value=\}[
][^\}
]+?;[
] />; 		<property name=message value=This block should be separated with blank line. />; 	</module>; ; 2 and 3 was not so easy to check with checkstyle but of course Eclipse formatter is more powerful in handling such cases; unfortunately I havent worked with Eclipse and formatter code base to develop it myself.; ; These simple rules should quickly make code more readable for those who has hard time reading spaghetti code often; especially after beginners.; ; I would gladfuly test this enhancement if any part of it is implemented.; ; Reproducible: Always

